is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables FORWARD chain on router6, applied to inbound traffic on `eth2` (the `r6_r3` uplink to router3 and the rest of the network).

**Mechanism:** Router6 has a set of iptables FORWARD rules that DROP all TCP, UDP, and ICMP traffic entering on `eth2`, with a single narrow exception (TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1). Any packet destined for the 172.18.51.0/24 or 172.18.50.0/24 subnets that arrives from the network side (via router3 on eth2) is silently discarded. Equally, any response packet returning from those subnets toward external hosts must re-enter router6 on eth2, so it too is dropped — this is why PC15–PC18 cannot successfully ping external hosts even though their outbound packets leave router6 normally. Router6 itself can reach the outside (e.g., 192.168.200.13) because its own self-originated pings traverse the INPUT/OUTPUT chains, not FORWARD. The ICMP DROP counter already shows 26 packets silently discarded, confirming the rule is actively firing.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, `eth2` (10.50.1.2, the link to router3).

**Root cause:** Restrictive iptables FORWARD DROP rules on router6's eth2 interface block all return and inbound traffic between the 172.18.51.x/172.18.50.x subnets and the rest of the topology, completely isolating PC15–PC18 and making the 172.18.51.x subnets unreachable from any site.

**Fix:** Remove (or correct) the DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain on eth2: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p icmp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`. Then persist the corrected ruleset.
=== END ===